  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how a triboelectric nanogenerator (TENG) and a MOSFET work by using charge separation and electric fields, highlighting a conceptual similarity between the two devices. On the left, the TENG is drawn as two stacked dielectric layers between electrodes connected to a load. As the layers move relative to each other, the alignment of positive and negative surface charges changes, creating a potential difference that drives current through the external circuit. The arrows around the sequence indicate repeated mechanical motion and alternating charge flow, showing how sliding or displacement generates electrical output.\n\nOn the right, the MOSFET is shown with a p-type substrate, n-type source and drain, and a gate separated by an insulator. In the upper sketch, charges in the semiconductor are distributed without a clear conducting path. In the lower sketch, an applied gate voltage attracts carriers and forms an n-type channel under the gate, connecting source and drain. Arrows indicate electron transport through this induced channel and current in the outer circuit. Overall, the figure emphasizes that both systems rely on charge redistribution to control electrical conduction, though one is mechanically driven and the other voltage controlled.",
